# robot_sw/.dockerignore — keep a developer's working-tree noise OUT of the app image.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS (measured 2026-09-01). `docker build -t go2-app:local robot_sw/` is only
# worth documenting if the image a developer builds equals the image CI builds. It did
# not: building from a working tree produced an image whose /opt/go2_ws/src held 35 files,
# while building the same commit from a clean export held 27 — the extra 8 were
# `__pycache__/*.pyc` and `.pytest_cache/` left behind by running the unit tests. They are
# in .gitignore, so git never showed them, and they rode into the image anyway.
#
# Nothing here is load-bearing for the build: the image's ROS packages are built by colcon
# INSIDE the container, so caches from the host's own python are noise at best and a stale
# .pyc shadowing a source file at worst.
#
# ⚠ THE PATTERNS NEED `**/`, AND THAT IS NOT A STYLE CHOICE. A .dockerignore is NOT a
# .gitignore: `__pycache__/` matches only the CONTEXT ROOT's directory, so the first
# version of this file (copied from ../.gitignore) excluded nothing at all — measured, the
# image still carried all 8 junk files. `**/__pycache__` is what reaches
# src/go2_detector/go2_detector/__pycache__.
#
**/__pycache__
**/*.py[cod]
**/.pytest_cache
**/*.egg-info

# colcon outputs, if someone built in-tree before docker build
**/build
**/install
**/log

# The locomotion policy is loaded by the simulator that moves the legs, never by this app.
models/
